qn 1- Reverse an array

Use the two-pointer technique.
- One pointer starts at index 0.
- Another pointer starts at the last index.
- Swap both elements.
- Move the pointers towards each other.
- Stop when they meet.

import java.util.Arrays;

public class ReverseArray {

    public static void main(String[] args) {

        int[] arr = {10, 20, 30, 40, 50};

        int left = 0;
        int right = arr.length - 1;

        while (left < right) {

            int temp = arr[left];

            arr[left] = arr[right];

            arr[right] = temp;

            left++;
            right--;
        }

        System.out.println(Arrays.toString(arr));
    }
}



java8

int[] arr = {10, 20, 30, 40, 50};

int[] reversed = IntStream.range(0, arr.length)
        .map(i -> arr[arr.length - 1 - i])
        .toArray();

System.out.println(Arrays.toString(reversed));



E. Complexity
Solution	Time	Extra space
Two pointers	O(n)	O(1)
Java 8	O(n)	O(n)

F. Choice of solution
Two pointers are better because they reverse the original array in place.

The Java 8 version creates a new array.



qn 2- Remove duplicates from an array

A LinkedHashSet is suitable because:
- It does not allow duplicate elements.
- It preserves insertion order.

plain java
int[] arr = {1, 2, 3, 2, 4, 3, 5};

Set<Integer> set = new LinkedHashSet<>();

for (int num : arr) {
    set.add(num);
}

int[] result = new int[set.size()];

int index = 0;

for (int num : set) {
    result[index++] = num;
}

System.out.println(Arrays.toString(result));



java 8
int[] arr = {1, 2, 3, 2, 4, 3, 5};

int[] result = Arrays.stream(arr)
        .distinct()
        .toArray();

System.out.println(Arrays.toString(result));

D. Complexity
Solution	Time	Extra space
LinkedHashSet	O(n) average	O(n)
Java 8 distinct	O(n) average	O(n)

E. Choice of solution
Use LinkedHashSet for explicit collection-based logic.

Use distinct() when writing Java 8 Stream code.


qn 3- Move all zeros to the end of an array

It represents the next position where a non-zero element should be placed.
1. Traverse the array.
2. Whenever a non-zero element is found, place it at arr[index].
3. Increment index.
4. After processing all non-zero elements, fill the remaining positions with zero.

int[] arr = {0, 1, 0, 3, 12};

int index = 0;

for (int num : arr) {

    if (num != 0) {
        arr[index++] = num;
    }
}

while (index < arr.length) {
    arr[index++] = 0;
}

System.out.println(Arrays.toString(arr));


java 8
-------
int[] arr = {0, 1, 0, 3, 12};

int[] result = IntStream.concat(
        Arrays.stream(arr).filter(n -> n != 0),
        Arrays.stream(arr).filter(n -> n == 0)
).toArray();

System.out.println(Arrays.toString(result));


E. Complexity
Solution	Time	Extra space
In-place approach	O(n)	O(1)
Java 8	O(n)	O(n)

F. Choice of solution
Use the in-place approach.

It is optimal in terms of time and auxiliary space.


qn 4 - Find the missing number in an array

sum = n[n+1]/2
sum = 6[7+2]/21
1+2+4+5+6=18
21-18=3

plain java
--------\
int[] arr = {1, 2, 4, 5, 6};

int n = 6;

int expectedSum = n * (n + 1) / 2;

int actualSum = 0;

for (int num : arr) {
    actualSum += num;
}

System.out.println(expectedSum - actualSum);

java 8
---------
int[] arr = {1, 2, 4, 5, 6};

int n = 6;

int actualSum = Arrays.stream(arr).sum();

int expectedSum = IntStream.rangeClosed(1, n).sum();

System.out.println(expectedSum - actualSum);

D. Complexity
Solution	Time	Extra space
Plain Java	O(n)	O(1)
Java 8	O(n)	O(1) auxiliary space

E. Choice of solution
Both approaches are efficient.

Use the mathematical formula when the input constraints are clear.

For very large integer ranges, consider long to prevent integer overflow.


